\use{book}

\title{The texish Reference Manual}
\author{The texish project}
\date{Version 0.21.1}

\frontmatter
\maketitle

\chapter{Preface}

This is the reference manual for \italic{texish}, a typesetting engine in the
tradition of Donald Knuth's \TeX. Like \TeX, texish reads a source document
written in a language of ordinary text interspersed with \italic{control
sequences}, and from it builds finished pages: lines broken and justified by the
Knuth--Plass algorithm, words divided by Liang's hyphenation patterns, formulae
set in real math mode, and the whole assembled by a page builder into a PDF.

The manual is written in two voices at once. Most of it is a narrative that
introduces the engine one idea at a time, in roughly the order you meet those
ideas when you write a document: characters and groups, then boxes and glue,
then paragraphs, then mathematics, and so on. The later part is a reference: a
systematic catalogue of the primitives and the bundled packages, to be consulted
rather than read. A reader new to texish should follow the narrative; a reader
who already knows what to do and needs to recall how should turn to the
reference.

A word on how to read the examples. Wherever the text shows you a piece of
source, it is set in a typewriter face exactly as you would type it:

\begin{verbatim}
Hello, world. This is $E = mc^2$.
\end{verbatim}

When a single control sequence is named in the middle of a sentence --- the
\verb|\section| command, say, or the \verb|\frac| of a fraction --- it too is
set in typewriter, so that it can never be mistaken for the surrounding prose.
Names of commands always begin with a backslash; names of packages and
environments do not.

This manual was, of course, typeset by texish itself.

\contents

\mainmatter

\part{Foundations}

\chapter{Introduction}

texish is a small program with a large ancestry. Its purpose is to turn a text
file into a beautifully set document, and almost everything about how it does so
is borrowed, deliberately, from \TeX: the box-and-glue model of layout, the
badness-driven line breaker, the math italic and the spacing rules of
mathematics, the idea that a document is a program in a macro language. What
texish adds is a modern home for those ideas --- it is written in Scala, runs on
the Java virtual machine, in the browser, and as a native executable, and draws
its pages through the Cairo graphics library --- and a deliberately small,
readable core that a student can hope to understand in full.

\section{What texish is}

At heart texish is an \italic{interpreter}. You give it a source document; it
reads that document from beginning to end, and as it reads it acts: it sets
characters into words, gathers words into paragraphs, breaks paragraphs into
lines, stacks lines and other material into pages, and ships each finished page
to the output. There is no separate parsing stage that builds a tree for a later
stage to walk; reading and typesetting happen together, in one pass over the
input, exactly as in \TeX. This is worth stating plainly because it explains a
good deal of the engine's behaviour: a command takes effect at the moment it is
read, and its effect can depend on everything read before it.

The output is a PDF (or, from the raster backends, a PNG). texish carries its own
fonts --- the Latin Modern family that descends from Knuth's Computer Modern, a
matching math font, and a monospace face for code --- so a document sets the same
way on every machine, with no external font installation.

\section{How a document becomes a page}

It helps to know the path your source takes through the engine, because the rest
of this manual is organised along it. There are four stages, each feeding the
next.

\subsection{Tokens}

First the source is broken into \italic{tokens}. A run of ordinary letters is one
text token; a backslash followed by letters is a control-sequence token, such as
\verb|\section|; the special characters --- the braces that open and close a
group, the \verb|$| that toggles mathematics --- are tokens of their own. A few
characters are \italic{active} and behave like commands in their own right; the tie
\verb|~|, which sets an unbreakable space, is one. Two slashes begin a comment
that runs to the end of the line. (Unlike \TeX, the percent sign is an ordinary
character in texish, free to be used in prose and in arithmetic.)

\subsection{Processing and expansion}

The tokens are then processed in order. Most control sequences are
\italic{primitives}, built into the engine: \verb|\hskip| inserts horizontal space,
\verb|\vbox| builds a vertical box, \verb|\section| opens a section. Others are
\italic{macros}, defined in the source or by a package with \verb|\def|; when the
engine meets a macro it replaces it with its definition and goes on reading. The
bundled packages --- loaded with \verb|\use| --- are nothing more than
collections of such definitions; the \verb|document| package, for instance,
defines \verb|\section| and the rest of the article furniture in terms of the
engine's primitives.

\subsection{Boxes and glue}

As the engine reads, the material it produces is assembled out of \italic{boxes}
and \italic{glue}, the two ideas at the centre of the whole system. A box is a
rectangle with a definite height, depth, and width --- a single character is a
box, a word is a box of boxes, a line is a wider one. Glue is stretchable,
shrinkable space set between boxes; it is glue that lets a line of type spread to
fill the measure, and glue that opens up the space above a heading. Paragraphs
are broken into lines by choosing breakpoints that make the glue stretch and
shrink as little as possible, the optimisation that gives texish its even
``colour'' on the page.

\subsection{Pages}

Finally the page builder collects the vertical list of lines, headings, and
spaces, finds good places to break it into pages, attaches the running head and
the folio, and ships each page to the PDF. Mathematics, footnotes, figures, and
multiple columns all feed into this same machinery.

\section{Your first document}

A texish document can be as short as a line. Put the following in a file called
\verb|hello.script|:

\begin{verbatim}
\use{document}

\section{Hello}

Hello, world. This is a paragraph of text, and here is a
displayed formula:
$$E = mc^2.$$

\vfill\eject
\end{verbatim}

The first line loads the \verb|document| package, which gives the article format:
sections, a title, lists, and so on. The body is ordinary text; a blank line ends
one paragraph and begins the next, just as in \TeX. The \verb|$$...$$| pair sets a
formula on a line of its own. The final \verb|\vfill\eject| fills the rest of the
last page with blank space and ships it.

To turn the source into a PDF, run the command-line tool from the repository:

\begin{verbatim}
sbt "texishCli/run hello.script"
\end{verbatim}

This writes \verb|hello.pdf| beside the source. That is the whole cycle: edit the
script, run the tool, read the PDF.

\section{How this manual is organized}

The narrative proceeds from the small to the large. After this introduction,
Part~I continues with the units of input --- characters, groups, and spaces ---
and then with the boxes and glue from which everything is built. Part~II covers
the horizontal world: words, paragraphs, line breaking, and hyphenation.
Part~III is devoted to mathematics, the largest single subject in the engine.
Part~IV describes the vertical world and the page builder, including footnotes,
columns, and floats. Part~V documents the bundled packages --- \verb|document|,
\verb|book|, \verb|math|, and the rest --- as the formats a working document
actually uses.

The reference that closes the manual lists every primitive with its arguments and
a one-line description, grouped by the chapter that explains it, so that a command
met in the narrative can be looked up later in a moment.

\vfill\eject
